ial stability, and the issuing of the national currency, the South African Rand. The SARB's inception came at a pivotal time for South Africa, following the Union of South Africa's establishment in 1910, marking a transition towards a more centralized financial system. Its founding was influenced by the economic realities of the post-World War I period and the need for a stable currency in a country facing economic challenges and increasing political complexity.

